    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "right, that makes sense. i'm looking at the activation maps now and it seems like the clusters responsible for the topological phase detection are deeply integrated with the underlying lattice dynamics weights. if i prune those \"specialized\" clusters like you're suggesting, i'm worried we'll lose the entire breakthrough. basically, the model will just be a standard physics LLM and won't be able to replicate the discovery at all. isn't there a way to keep the discovery intact without the heavy weight-set?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Instead of full pruning, consider knowledge distillation or weight regularization targeted only at the high-frequency features associated with the discovery. You could then inject the essence of the specialized cluster's function into a smaller, less specialized network via a soft-target loss function. This allows the baseline model to mimic the *decision* of the complex model without containing the specific, over-fitted weight structures derived from your fine-tuning.",
      "source": "target",
